    <section class="msc-learn-key-terms"><h2>Key Terms</h2><dl><div class="msc-learn-term"><dt>Bitcoin</dt><dd>An open monetary network and asset governed by independently verified consensus rules.</dd></div>
<div class="msc-learn-term"><dt>Wallet</dt><dd>Software or hardware that manages keys, constructs transactions, and helps users interact with Bitcoin.</dd></div>
<div class="msc-learn-term"><dt>Self-Custody</dt><dd>Direct control of the keys needed to spend bitcoin.</dd></div>
<div class="msc-learn-term"><dt>Private key</dt><dd>Secret cryptographic material used to authorize spending.</dd></div>
<div class="msc-learn-term"><dt>Seed phrase</dt><dd>A human-readable backup representation used by many wallets to derive keys under a wallet standard.</dd></div>
<div class="msc-learn-term"><dt>Transaction</dt><dd>A data structure that spends existing UTXOs and creates new outputs.</dd></div>
<div class="msc-learn-term"><dt>UTXO</dt><dd>An unspent transaction output that can be used as an input in a later transaction.</dd></div>
<div class="msc-learn-term"><dt>Confirmation</dt><dd>The inclusion of a transaction in a block, followed by additional blocks that increase settlement depth.</dd></div>
<div class="msc-learn-term"><dt>Halving</dt><dd>The scheduled reduction in Bitcoin&#39;s block subsidy after each 210,000-block interval.</dd></div>
<div class="msc-learn-term"><dt>Block subsidy</dt><dd>New bitcoin created under the issuance schedule and paid through a block&#39;s coinbase transaction.</dd></div>
<div class="msc-learn-term"><dt>Bitcoin privacy</dt><dd>The degree to which transaction activity, ownership relationships, and user identity can remain difficult to observe or connect.</dd></div></dl></section>
